# BIG-IP Pool Member P-521 Certificate Chain Auditor

[![Python Version](https://img.shields.io/badge/python-3.8%2B-blue.svg)](https://www.python.org/)
[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](LICENSE)
[![F5 TMOS](https://img.shields.io/badge/F5%20TMOS-v14.x%20--%20v17.x-red.svg)](https://www.f5.com)
[![Code Style: Black](https://img.shields.io/badge/code%20style-black-000000.svg)](https://github.com/psf/black)

A multi-threaded security and compliance auditing tool designed for F5 BIG-IP network and systems engineers. This tool parses `bigip.conf` configurations, extracts all backend LTM pool members, queries their live TLS endpoints, and detects whether any certificate in the presented chain (leaf, intermediate CA, or root CA) utilizes an Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) key based on the NIST P-521 curve (`secp521r1` / OID `1.3.132.0.35`).

---

## Background & Problem Statement

In enterprise application delivery environments, backend SSL/TLS offload profiles (`serverssl`) are responsible for re-encrypting traffic between the BIG-IP appliance and backend pool members. 

While modern TLS stacks support Elliptic Curve Cryptography, **NIST P-521 (`secp521r1`)** introduces significant operational risks on application delivery controllers:

1. **Hardware Acceleration Bottlenecks**: Hardware crypto offload engines (e.g., Cavium/Nitrox acceleration cards found on BIG-IP iSeries appliances and VIPRION blades) natively accelerate standard curves like P-256 (`secp256r1`) and P-384 (`secp384r1`). Certificates signed by CAs using P-521 keys frequently cannot be offloaded to hardware, triggering software crypto fallback or handshake aborts.
2. **TMOS Handshake Failures**: If the `serverssl` profile does not explicitly allow 521-bit curve negotiation or if strict cipher suites are enforced, backend handshakes fail with `SSL_HANDSHAKE_FAILURE (40)`, causing monitors to mark pool members down.
3. **Internal PKI Blindspots**: While leaf certificates might be standard RSA 2048 or ECDSA P-256, internal enterprise Certificate Authorities (CAs) or intermediate CAs occasionally issue certificates under high-bitwidth curves without operations teams knowing until outages occur.

This script automates the discovery of affected backend pool members before firmware upgrades or security policy enforcements take place.

---

## Features

- **Robust TMOS Stanza Parser**: Recursively extracts nested `ltm pool` and `members { ... }` blocks across all partitions (`/Common/`, administrative partitions) using balanced-brace tokenization.
- **Deep Chain Inspection**: Captures the entire certificate chain returned in the TLS ServerHello (leaf, intermediate CAs, and root certificates) without terminating on trust store verification errors (ideal for internal, private, or self-signed PKI).
- **Exact Curve Detection**: Inspects X.509 v3 public key parameters and ASN.1 OIDs to identify `secp521r1`, `nistp521`, `p-521`, `prime521v1`, and OID `1.3.132.0.35`.
- **Concurrent Scanning**: Multi-threaded execution pool allows scanning hundreds of pool members in seconds with customizable thread counts and socket timeouts.
- **SNI Support**: Allows sending custom Server Name Indication (SNI) extensions during the TLS ClientHello for environments with multi-tenant virtual hosting.
- **CI/CD & Automation Ready**: Supports both human-readable terminal output and machine-parseable JSON export.

